specially important  here there are no exploited voices to  cry allowed  no oppressed merc  persons to march in our streets  to protest  to act in political rebellion  we can if we will continue to ignore all  of this  continue to preserve pursue our glut of  consumers goods  and  die  our rationality  our ideals our good intentions  being ruled by our greed  how then do fundamental spiritual  attitudes change  for on them we have suggested  depends the possibility of new moral  obligations  commitment and choices  and on these latter depend the  possibilities  of new public  or practical policies towards nature  both religion and science are part of  the problem  as i've tried to note  let us see how they might help in these  changes of attitude  attitudes and moral obligations  represent needless to say  especially the area for which religion  feels responsible we will therefore  begin there  i wish to suggest an obvious but  slightly heretical notion perhaps most  creative notions are heretical i don't  know  history of theology seems to hint that  if not say it  we should proclaim and teach that nature  is an image of god  imago dei  a creature that is also a sign or symbol  of god as are we  hence nature has value in and for itself  as do we  an inherent integrity order and worth  that we must respect  as we do in the case of another person  nature is a thou as the great martin  buber said  an image of its creator and thus not  merely our instrument or material  but a genuine other to us  nature shows forth mirrors as an image  the glory the power the wisdom the  infinity  of its creator and preserver  in fact the god of the hebrew and the  christian scriptures  is one imaged continually  and conceived through  the signs and traces provided by the  grandeur  the power the immensity  the order  and the beauty of nature  it is through analogies from nature  as well as from persons community and  history that god becomes god  nature is not only a sign of god  it is also of ultimate concern to us  for the nature is the source of all life  and so of the future of life  nature is as nothing else is our mother  and creator  we not only tower over nature as  observers and users  as scientists and technologists  and vacationers  as creaturely humans we all are also  absolutely dependent on nature  thanks to evolutionary science  we now know this in a quite new way  namely  that it is through nature  that god has brought us into being  and preserves us there  interestingly and ironically we have  returned through scientific inquiry in  our own age  to this knowledge possessed and  celebrated  by all primordial religions  let me reiterate this  our change scientific understanding of  our origins  has transformed or should do so  our sense of our dependence on nature  heretofore most western men and women  have recognized their dependence in the  present on nature for air for food for  rain and so on and on  but traditionally in our minds  the creation of men and women by god  was seen as a quite separate event  from the creation of nature by god as so  to speak independent or separate  creation  but now  believing jews and christians who also  accept as do most of us in this room  the evolutionary account of our origins  in nature  we now understand god's creative work in  a quite different way  and this can be emphasized in all of our  religious groups  god has created us as humans through  nature  through the long slow processes of  development in nature  the long cosmic geological and finally  biological processes  that are produced first are cosmos  then our world then life and finally  ourselves  a process of creation though without its  theological thrills elegantly described  by professor sagan  in this sense  as for the earliest religions nature is  in fact our mother  the instrument god has used to produce  us  much as god produces each one of us  through our own mother and father  our dependence on the divine for our  existence in life  is directly expressed in our dependence  on nature  as primordial religion knew  in nature lie the sacred powers of life  of fertility  of community  of health  and of security  as well as the powers  and order of existence  both our scientific and our religious  inheritance have heretofore  been quite blind to this  one final word then about science  reality and nature  as we have seen as the creative source  of our existence or being  in all its facets  nature as infinite as power as order  life and beauty  the reality of nature far transcends  the inert determined external vacuous  system of objects  pictured by empirical science  although that scientific description is  itself alive  with deeper implications of mystery  implications of spontaneity of novelty  of random changes evolving into life  with its self-direction its purposes its  feelings  reactions and finally consciousness and  self-consciousness  nature reveals itself in us  as living subject  it reveals itself in us  as being as power and as life  and it reveals itself around us  as  strategic power  and order  it is not only an object of our  inquiries it is also the living source  of our being  including the being of the objective  scientific inquirer  who looked at and defined  nature  nature thus manifests itself to us  as more than all we can know  or ever could know of it  it manifests itself in ourselves as  subjects  as well as studied objects  as a mystery in part  only fathomed by our empirical  investigation  science represents a wonderfully  accurate  but nonetheless limited perspective  on this wondrous mystery of the universe  and the cosmos  the reality of nature creates us  supports us lures us  it offers itself to our inquiries and to  our youth  but it eludes our final understanding  as it will elude our final domination  this sense of nature's mystery of its  sacred mystery  and of its depth  integrity and richness  and inherent value  can well supplement our attempts to  objectify  and so to use nature  thus can a renewed chastened and humbled  science  like a renewed and humbled religion  become a participant  in the homage to nature  rather than the cognitive basis for its  exploitation  and only thus in chastened partnership  can we begin to control that  exploitation  so as to rescue nature from ourselves  her children thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

